    "summary": "Strong post‑comeback set with fiery Sugaree → Jack Straw and soaring Stella Blue",
    "blurb": "The first set closes tight on a seamless Sugaree → Jack Straw transition, while the second set delivers high‑energy Willie And The Hand Jive into Iko Iko and an emotional Stella Blue. Jerry’s vocals and guitar are freshly invigorated after his return, and the band locks in on Crazy Fingers, Estimated Prophet and Sugar Magnolia.",
    "review": "December 16, 1986 at the Oakland‑Alameda County Coliseum found the Dead riding a wave of renewed energy following Jerry Garcia’s comeback from his coma. The night opened with a gritty Hell In A Bucket and moved through familiar terrain before striking gold on the Sugaree → Jack Straw segue – a moment many reviewers called “fiery” and “tight”. The second set kept the momentum rolling: Box of Rain gave way to a punchy Crazy Fingers, which segued into an inspired Estimated Prophet and a soaring Eyes Of The World. A highlight was the drum‑driven burst into Willie And The Hand Jive that erupted into an exuberant Iko Iko, capped by an emotionally charged Stella Blue that had the crowd hanging on every note. The band’s chemistry felt fresh; Jerry’s voice rang clear, Bob stepped forward in the mix delivering standout work on Crazy Fingers and Estimated Prophet, while Brent’s keys provided the usual textural depth. Though a few recordings note tape‑speed quirks and an uneven mix on some SBD sources, the best matrix capture (gd1986-12-16.sbd.miller.77409.flac16) presents a clean, well‑balanced sound that lets these moments shine.\n\nFor Deadheads seeking the definitive listening experience, the Miller matrix recording is the clear choice – it balances the energy of the live show with a ~75 % soundboard mix and crisp separation, preserving the nuance of Jerry’s guitar and the high‑octane jam sections. Even listeners limited to audience tapes can still appreciate the raw power of Crazy Fingers and the joyous charge of Sugar Magnolia, but the matrix version offers the most complete and sonically satisfying portrait of a post‑comeback night that truly felt like a fresh start for the band.",
